WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.020
Modularity matters when it changes an operational decision.

2
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:07.560
If equipment can evolve without replacing the supporting infrastructure, an operator may

3
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:10.660
extend useful life or choose between qualified suppliers.

4
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:15.000
Shared interfaces also make repair and staged upgrades easier to plan.

5
00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.900
These benefits are conditional.

6
00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.820
A replacement part must meet the relevant mechanical, electrical, thermal and management

7
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:22.380
requirements.

8
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.200
A later revision can change behavior even when the connector still fits.

9
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:31.880
Reuse therefore needs condition assessment and documented compatibility, not just the absence

10
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:32.740
of visible damage.

11
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.260
Consider replacing one equipment generation while retaining the rack and power distribution.

12
00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:39.720
Mechanical fit is only the first check.

13
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:43.480
The new equipment may draw a different peak current, reject heat differently

14
00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.120
or need additional telemetry.

15
00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:49.520
The retained infrastructure must support the new envelope, including the agreed fault

16
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:49.840
case.

17
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:53.220
Treat modularity as a way to create options with evidence.

18
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.300
It is not permission to mix unqualified assemblies, and it doesn't remove

19
00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.940
the need to control revisions throughout the deployment's life.

